                <head>173. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>At the China Committee meeting of May 27, the British Delegate stated that his
                    government had decided to eliminate the China differential. The text of his
                    statement was transmitted to the Department in Polto 2828 from Paris, May 27.
                    (Department of State, Central Files, 493.009/5–2757) On May 29 Prime Minister
                    Macmillan explained the British decision in a statement to the House of Commons.
                    The British Foreign Office provided the Embassy in London with an advance text
                    of Macmillan’s statement, which it transmitted to the Department in telegram
                    6546 from London, May 29. (<hi rendition="#italic">Ibid.,</hi>
                    493.009/5–2957)</p>
                <p>On May 30, the Department of State issued a press release expressing its
                    disappointment with the British decision. For text of the press release, see
                    Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, June 17, 1957, pages
                    967–968.</p>
            </div>
